data: {"run_id": "research-0001", "status": "written", "brief": {"subject": {"project_id": "P156634", "project_name": "Water and Sanitation Development Project", "closing_date": "2026-10-31", "as_of": "2026-11-05"}, "headline": {"index": 0, "text": "In the last months before its closing date, the project's Baricho works on the coast were finishing while contracts were still being awarded, and residents in Mombasa had pushed back on its works.", "kind": "summary", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4", "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/a230f243be70415c"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "headline", "group": null, "part": "sentence"}, "picture": [{"index": 1, "text": "The Baricho solar plant, financed under the project at Sh670 million, was 60 percent complete in January 2026 and 90 per cent complete in March 2026, and the World Bank's report of June 30, 2026 says its solarization is complete, with savings estimated at Kshs 10 million per month.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93", "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4"], "numbers": [{"text": "670", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3"}, {"text": "60", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3"}, {"text": "90", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93"}, {"text": "10", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3"}], "dates": [{"text": "January 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3"}, {"text": "March 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3"}, {"text": "June 30, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4"}], "quote": null, "section": "picture", "group": 0, "part": "sentence", "paragraph": 0}, {"index": 2, "text": "The agency is seeking additional partners and donors for phase two, which will extend solar-powered water pumping to Mombasa County.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"], "numbers": [{"text": "two", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"}], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "picture", "group": 0, "part": "sentence", "paragraph": 0}, {"index": 3, "text": "In Wajir, the faecal sludge treatment plant meant to end the town's bucket toilets was 64% complete on June 4, 2026, when the Water and Sanitation Secretary urged the contractor and project consultant to accelerate construction works.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947"], "numbers": [{"text": "64", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947"}], "dates": [{"text": "June 4, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947"}], "quote": null, "section": "picture", "group": 0, "part": "sentence", "paragraph": 0}, {"index": 4, "text": "Between July 15, 2026 and October 13, 2026, the project's agencies published six contract awards in the 110 days before the closing date, two of them by direct selection, the last 18 days before closing.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05", "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00456907", "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474946"], "numbers": [{"text": "six", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}, {"text": "110", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}, {"text": "two", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}, {"text": "18", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}], "dates": [{"text": "July 15, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00456907"}, {"text": "October 13, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}], "quote": null, "section": "picture", "group": 1, "part": "sentence", "paragraph": 1}, {"index": 5, "text": "In Mombasa, Old Town traders stormed the water company's offices in October 2022 over delayed disturbance allowances, and Mwembe Tayari residents voiced outrage over job promises and disruptions.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/a230f243be70415c"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "October 2022", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835"}], "quote": null, "section": "picture", "group": 1, "part": "sentence", "paragraph": 1}, {"index": 6, "text": "The Kipevu wastewater plant, which was being rehabilitated at Sh280 million in May 2022, was still under rehabilitation in the World Bank's data of January 30, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b", "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7"], "numbers": [{"text": "280", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b"}], "dates": [{"text": "May 2022", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b"}, {"text": "January 30, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7"}], "quote": null, "section": "picture", "group": 1, "part": "sentence", "paragraph": 1}], "bottom_line": [{"index": 7, "text": "With six contracts awarded in the 110 days before closing, two by direct selection, check each contract's completion date against the closing date of October 31, 2026.", "kind": "advice", "evidence_ids": ["strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"], "numbers": [{"text": "six", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}, {"text": "110", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}, {"text": "two", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}], "dates": [{"text": "October 31, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}], "quote": null, "section": "bottom_line", "group": null, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 8, "text": "The Kipevu plant was still under rehabilitation in January 2026, years after it was due, so ask for its extensions of time and the date it will be taken over.", "kind": "advice", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b", "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "January 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7"}], "quote": null, "section": "bottom_line", "group": null, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 9, "text": "The Old Town agreements on disturbance allowances and dust control are where residents have protested before, so ask the Mombasa water company what has been paid and done.", "kind": "advice", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "bottom_line", "group": null, "part": "sentence"}], "developments": [{"date": "2026-10-13", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 10, "text": "The award for equipping the Sarman - Tarbaj boreholes, Lot 1, was published on October 13, 2026, a day after the invitation for bids for the same works and 18 days before the closing date.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474946", "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474700", "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"], "numbers": [{"text": "1", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474946"}, {"text": "18", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}], "dates": [{"text": "October 13, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474946"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 0, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-10-12", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 11, "text": "The award for equipping the Qaras boreholes, Lot 3, was published on October 12, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474696"], "numbers": [{"text": "3", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474696"}], "dates": [{"text": "October 12, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474696"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 1, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 12, "text": "A civil society lobby demanded a solution to the Changamwe-Jomvu water crisis in Mombasa on October 8, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/b87138d428e8a344"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "October 8, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/b87138d428e8a344"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 2, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 13, "text": "The solar system for the Ukunda water supply was awarded by direct selection on September 25, 2026, for KES 314838645.42.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125"], "numbers": [{"text": "314838645.42", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125"}], "dates": [{"text": "September 25, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 3, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-09-04", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 14, "text": "The supply and installation of additional pumps at Baricho was awarded on September 4, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00466974"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "September 4, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00466974"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 4, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-07-20", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 15, "text": "The Vindo booster station works were awarded by direct selection on July 20, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00457686"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "July 20, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00457686"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 5, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-07-15", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 16, "text": "The equipping of the Nurgows boreholes, Lot 2, was awarded on July 15, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00456907"], "numbers": [{"text": "2", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00456907"}], "dates": [{"text": "July 15, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00456907"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 6, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-06-30", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 17, "text": "The World Bank's report of June 30, 2026 says the Baricho rehabilitation added 36,000m³/day of supply capacity to Kilifi and Mombasa counties, and that the Garissa and Wajir boreholes and sanitation facilities are \"substantially complete or nearing finalization\".", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4"], "numbers": [{"text": "36,000", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4"}], "dates": [{"text": "June 30, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 7, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-06-04", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 18, "text": "The Wajir faecal sludge treatment plant was 64% complete, and the Water and Sanitation Secretary urged the contractor and project consultant to accelerate construction works.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947"], "numbers": [{"text": "64", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947"}], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 8, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-03-20", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 19, "text": "The World Bank approved the Coastal Region Water Security and Climate Resilience Project (P513697) on March 20, 2026, to increase bulk water supply to Mombasa County and Kwale County.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/record-P513697"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "March 20, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/record-P513697"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 9, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-03-13", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 20, "text": "Inspecting Baricho, the Cabinet Secretary said the solar project was at 90 per cent complete, with 6,716 solar panels installed.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93"], "numbers": [{"text": "90", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93"}, {"text": "6,716", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93"}], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 10, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 21, "text": "The World Bank's report of March 12, 2026 put overall physical progress of works contracts at 85 percent, with 46 percent of works contracts and all goods contracts completed.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p2-s4"], "numbers": [{"text": "85", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p2-s4"}, {"text": "46", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p2-s4"}], "dates": [{"text": "March 12, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p2-s4"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 10, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2026-01-20", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 22, "text": "The Baricho solar plant, begun in October 2025 and due by March 31, 2026, was 60 percent complete, and the agency paid about Sh60 million a month for power.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"], "numbers": [{"text": "60", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3"}, {"text": "60", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3"}], "dates": [{"text": "October 2025", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3"}, {"text": "March 31, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 11, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2025-05-16", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 23, "text": "Launching the Northern Water Works Development Agency's plan for 2024 to 2028, the Cabinet Secretary said the ministry had invested more than Sh12 billion in the region.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"], "numbers": [{"text": "12", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"}], "dates": [{"text": "2024", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"}, {"text": "2028", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 12, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2025-01-25", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 24, "text": "The Governor of Taita Taveta called for the Coast Water Works Development Agency to be disbanded for failing to supply water to the region.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/c1f6a8f12b71bd4a"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 13, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2024-11-21", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 25, "text": "People Daily reported that a report by the Auditor General named the Coast Water Works Development Agency \"the most incompetent agency in the water sector with almost all the projects delayed in implementation\".", "kind": "quote", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": {"text": "the most incompetent agency in the water sector with almost all the projects delayed in implementation", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45"}, "section": "developments", "group": 14, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2024-06-15", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 26, "text": "The government announced the Sh4 billion Baricho water works complete, with three boreholes destroyed by floods in 2018 rehabilitated and the Baricho-Kakuyuni pipeline done for Sh2 billion.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"], "numbers": [{"text": "4", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"}, {"text": "three", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"}, {"text": "2", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"}], "dates": [{"text": "2018", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 15, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2022-10-28", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 27, "text": "Old Town representatives stormed the Mombasa Water Supply and Sanitation Company offices over delayed compensation, with at least 270 people affected and Sh6,000 settled as the amount each business would get.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835"], "numbers": [{"text": "270", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835"}, {"text": "6,000", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835"}], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 16, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": "2022-05-16", "date_known": true, "label": null, "sentences": [{"index": 28, "text": "The Kipevu wastewater treatment plant, constructed in 1980, was being rehabilitated at Sh280 million and \"is expected to be done by October this year\".", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b"], "numbers": [{"text": "280", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b"}], "dates": [{"text": "1980", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b"}], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 17, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"date": null, "date_known": false, "label": "undated", "sentences": [{"index": 29, "text": "Mwembe Tayari residents voiced outrage over job promises and disruptions from a Mombasa water project.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/a230f243be70415c"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "developments", "group": 18, "part": "sentence"}]}], "by_site": [{"title": {"index": 30, "text": "On the coast, the Baricho works were finishing.", "kind": "summary", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93", "strata.research.item:P156634/sentinel2_mosaic/200577-2018-2025"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 0, "part": "title"}, "sentences": [{"index": 31, "text": "The project rehabilitated the Baricho water supply system, adding 36,000m³/day of supply capacity to Kilifi and Mombasa counties.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4"], "numbers": [{"text": "36,000", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4"}], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 0, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 32, "text": "The ministry announced the Sh4 billion Baricho water works complete in June 2024.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"], "numbers": [{"text": "4", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"}], "dates": [{"text": "June 2024", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 0, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 33, "text": "The solar plant of 4.6 megawatts on the 11 boreholes was financed at Sh670 million, and the agency expects to save at least Sh10 million per month in power bills.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"], "numbers": [{"text": "4.6", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"}, {"text": "11", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"}, {"text": "670", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"}, {"text": "10", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"}], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 0, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 34, "text": "Additional pumps for Baricho were awarded on September 4, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00466974"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "September 4, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00466974"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 0, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 35, "text": "People Daily reported in November 2024 that the Auditor General found the Baricho Well-Field Protection Works, allocated Sh 3.6 billion in 2021, still in progress.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45"], "numbers": [{"text": "3.6", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45"}], "dates": [{"text": "November 2024", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45"}, {"text": "2021", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 0, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 36, "text": "The Sentinel-2 cloudless mosaics of 2018 and 2025 around Baricho Sub-Location are set side by side for a person to compare.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/sentinel2_mosaic/200577-2018-2025"], "numbers": [{"text": "2", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/sentinel2_mosaic/200577-2018-2025"}], "dates": [{"text": "2018", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/sentinel2_mosaic/200577-2018-2025"}, {"text": "2025", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/sentinel2_mosaic/200577-2018-2025"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 0, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"title": {"index": 37, "text": "In Mombasa, residents have pushed back on the project's works.", "kind": "summary", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/a230f243be70415c"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 1, "part": "title"}, "sentences": [{"index": 38, "text": "In Old Town in 2022, residents waited for disturbance allowances while \"the old pipes spill sewage all over the place\".", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "2022", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 1, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 39, "text": "The Kipevu wastewater plant was still under rehabilitation in the World Bank's data of January 30, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "January 30, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 1, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 40, "text": "On October 8, 2026, a civil society lobby demanded a solution to the Changamwe-Jomvu water crisis.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/b87138d428e8a344"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "October 8, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/b87138d428e8a344"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 1, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"title": {"index": 41, "text": "Kwale's contracts were still to finish, and the project's two direct awards were a solar system for the Ukunda water supply and the Vindo booster station.", "kind": "summary", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7", "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05", "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125", "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00457686"], "numbers": [{"text": "two", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 2, "part": "title"}, "sentences": [{"index": 42, "text": "The World Bank's report of March 12, 2026 expects its water delivery targets \"to be exceeded once Kwale contracts are complete\".", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "March 12, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 2, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 43, "text": "The solar system for the Ukunda water supply was awarded by direct selection on September 25, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "September 25, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 2, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 44, "text": "The Vindo booster station works were awarded by direct selection on July 20, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00457686"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "July 20, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00457686"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 2, "part": "sentence"}]}, {"title": {"index": 45, "text": "In the north-east, Wajir's sludge treatment plant was still being built in June 2026, and the last boreholes were awarded in October 2026.", "kind": "summary", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947", "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474946"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "June 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947"}, {"text": "October 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474946"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 3, "part": "title"}, "sentences": [{"index": 46, "text": "Wajir's flat sandy terrain and high water table have made pit latrines unsuitable, and the new plant is about five kilometres from Wajir town.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947"], "numbers": [{"text": "five", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947"}], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 3, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 47, "text": "The World Bank's report of June 30, 2026 says the boreholes and sanitation facilities in Garissa and Wajir are substantially complete or nearing finalization.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "June 30, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 3, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 48, "text": "The World Bank's data of January 30, 2026 count 34 community water points so far, with additional source identification and drilling works ongoing.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p5-s7"], "numbers": [{"text": "34", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p5-s7"}], "dates": [{"text": "January 30, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p5-s7"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 3, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 49, "text": "Launching the northern agency's plan in May 2025, the Cabinet Secretary said \"the water connectivity on the ground tells a different story\".", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "May 2025", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"}], "quote": null, "section": "by_site", "group": 3, "part": "sentence"}]}], "voices": [{"quote": "We have had problems when water supply is disrupted because of power being disconnected due to unpaid bills", "who": "David Kanui, the water project coordinator at the Coast Water Works Development Agency", "outlet": "The Star", "date": "2026-01-14", "url": "https://www.the-star.co.ke/counties/coast/2026-01-14-solar-water-project-set-to-cut-power-costs-improve-supply-in-kilifi", "sentence": {"index": 50, "text": "David Kanui said: \"We have had problems when water supply is disrupted because of power being disconnected due to unpaid bills\".", "kind": "quote", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": {"text": "We have had problems when water supply is disrupted because of power being disconnected due to unpaid bills", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"}, "section": "voices", "group": 0, "part": "quote"}}, {"quote": "We have been dealing with high electricity bills because we are in low-lying areas and need to pump the water", "who": "Eric Murithi, Water, Sanitation and Irrigation Cabinet Secretary", "outlet": "The Standard", "date": "2026-03-13", "url": "https://www.standardmedia.co.ke/coast/article/2001542940/baricho-solarisation-project-promises-cheaper-water-for-the-coast", "sentence": {"index": 51, "text": "Eric Murithi said: \"We have been dealing with high electricity bills because we are in low-lying areas and need to pump the water\".", "kind": "quote", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": {"text": "We have been dealing with high electricity bills because we are in low-lying areas and need to pump the water", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93"}, "section": "voices", "group": 1, "part": "quote"}}, {"quote": "The project is good but it disrupted our businesses because of the construction works", "who": "Lubnar Omar, for the Old Town community in Mombasa", "outlet": "The Star", "date": "2022-10-28", "url": "https://www.the-star.co.ke/news/2022-10-28-mombasa-residents-protest-over-sewerage-renovation-payout-delays", "sentence": {"index": 52, "text": "Lubnar Omar said: \"The project is good but it disrupted our businesses because of the construction works\".", "kind": "quote", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": {"text": "The project is good but it disrupted our businesses because of the construction works", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835"}, "section": "voices", "group": 2, "part": "quote"}}, {"quote": "The Ministry has invested more than Sh12 billion in this region but the water connectivity on the ground tells a different story", "who": "Eric Mugaa, the Cabinet Secretary for Water, Sanitation and Irrigation", "outlet": "Kenya News Agency", "date": "2025-05-16", "url": "https://www.kenyanews.go.ke/?p=150106", "sentence": {"index": 53, "text": "Eric Mugaa said: \"The Ministry has invested more than Sh12 billion in this region but the water connectivity on the ground tells a different story\".", "kind": "quote", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"], "numbers": [{"text": "12", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"}], "dates": [], "quote": {"text": "The Ministry has invested more than Sh12 billion in this region but the water connectivity on the ground tells a different story", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"}, "section": "voices", "group": 3, "part": "quote"}}, {"quote": "There is an audit report that came from the national audit office looking at the water infrastructure in the region and the verdict is that we have invested in small water pans that end up not being sustainable", "who": "Julius Korir, Principal Secretary for Water", "outlet": "Kenya News Agency", "date": "2025-05-16", "url": "https://www.kenyanews.go.ke/?p=150106", "sentence": {"index": 54, "text": "Julius Korir said: \"There is an audit report that came from the national audit office looking at the water infrastructure in the region and the verdict is that we have invested in small water pans that end up not being sustainable\".", "kind": "quote", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": {"text": "There is an audit report that came from the national audit office looking at the water infrastructure in the region and the verdict is that we have invested in small water pans that end up not being sustainable", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f"}, "section": "voices", "group": 4, "part": "quote"}}], "questions": [{"lead": "The last awards.", "sentences": [{"index": 55, "text": "Ask which of the six contracts awarded in the 110 days before closing can be completed by the closing date of October 31, 2026, and which will run past it.", "kind": "advice", "evidence_ids": ["strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"], "numbers": [{"text": "six", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}, {"text": "110", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}], "dates": [{"text": "October 31, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 0, "part": "question"}, {"index": 56, "text": "The last award came 18 days before closing, a day after its invitation for bids was published.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05", "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474700", "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474946"], "numbers": [{"text": "18", "evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"}], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 0, "part": "question"}]}, {"lead": "The direct contracts.", "sentences": [{"index": 57, "text": "The Ukunda solar system and the Vindo booster station works were awarded by direct selection.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125", "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00457686"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 1, "part": "question"}, {"index": 58, "text": "Ask what justification is on file for each, and whether the Ukunda solar system repeats the Baricho solar design.", "kind": "advice", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125", "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00457686", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 1, "part": "question"}]}, {"lead": "Baricho well-field works.", "sentences": [{"index": 59, "text": "The ministry announced the Sh4 billion Baricho works complete in June 2024.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"], "numbers": [{"text": "4", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"}], "dates": [{"text": "June 2024", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"}], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 2, "part": "question"}, {"index": 60, "text": "Ask whether the final account is closed and the taking-over certificate has been issued.", "kind": "advice", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 2, "part": "question"}]}, {"lead": "Kipevu.", "sentences": [{"index": 61, "text": "The plant was expected to be done in 2022 and was still under rehabilitation on January 30, 2026.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b", "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7"], "numbers": [], "dates": [{"text": "2022", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b"}, {"text": "January 30, 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7"}], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 3, "part": "question"}, {"index": 62, "text": "Ask what extensions of time were granted, and whether the plant will be taken over before closing.", "kind": "advice", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7", "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 3, "part": "question"}]}, {"lead": "The Wajir sludge plant.", "sentences": [{"index": 63, "text": "With the plant at 64% in June 2026, ask whether it can be commissioned by the closing date, and if not, under which financing it finishes.", "kind": "advice", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947", "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"], "numbers": [{"text": "64", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947"}], "dates": [{"text": "June 2026", "evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947"}], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 4, "part": "question"}]}, {"lead": "Community agreements in Mombasa.", "sentences": [{"index": 64, "text": "Ask whether the Old Town disturbance allowances and the water bowsers agreed against dust are in the works contracts, and what has been paid.", "kind": "advice", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 5, "part": "question"}]}, {"lead": "The coast agency's standing.", "sentences": [{"index": 65, "text": "People Daily reported that the Auditor General named the coast agency the most incompetent agency in the water sector, and the Governor of Taita Taveta called for the agency to be disbanded.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/c1f6a8f12b71bd4a"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 6, "part": "question"}, {"index": 66, "text": "Ask how the coast agency will finish the works contracts still under way within the remaining project period.", "kind": "advice", "evidence_ids": ["strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p2-s4", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/c1f6a8f12b71bd4a"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "questions", "group": 6, "part": "question"}]}], "could_not_see": [{"index": 67, "text": "GDELT limited calls from this network, so the GDELT news index was not read for this brief.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "could_not_see", "group": null, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 68, "text": "Eastleigh Voice's page answered a bot check, so its item holds the headline only.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05", "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/a230f243be70415c"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "could_not_see", "group": null, "part": "sentence"}, {"index": 69, "text": "No case in the Inspection Panel's register names the project.", "kind": "record", "evidence_ids": ["strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05"], "numbers": [], "dates": [], "quote": null, "section": "could_not_see", "group": null, "part": "sentence"}], "evidence": {"strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40114539-p2-s4", "public_from": "2026-06-30", "source": {"key": "isr_narrative", "label": "World Bank project reports", "kind": "bank_report", "license": "The World Bank: Documents & Reports (documents.worldbank.org). License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentlist?projid=P156634&docty_exact=Implementation+Status+and+Results+Report"}, "kind": "bank_report", "title": "ISR ISR07733, June 30, 2026: Implementation Status and Key Decisions, page 2", "text": "The Project is making steady overall progress across its components. In the coastal region, bulk water supply capacity has been significantly enhanced through the rehabilitation of the Baricho water supply system, adding 36,000m³/day of supply capacity to Kilifi and Mombasa counties. Solarization of the Baricho bulk water system is complete and expected to deliver substantial energy cost savings estimated at Kshs 10 million per month. Water distribution and sanitation contracts are advancing well, with most works either completed or nearing completion. Sanitation infrastructure, including ablution blocks, sewer networks, and fecal sludge treatment plants, is largely on track. In the northeastern counties, rehabilitation and construction of boreholes and sanitation facilities in Garissa and Wajir are substantially complete or nearing finalization, and long-term water supply planning for Wajir county is advancing with key technical and hydrogeological deliverables are being finalized. At the national level, the Conditional Liquidity Support Grant is rolling out activities aimed at supporting the financial recovery of Water Service Providers. The Utility Turnaround Framework has supported tariff reviews and institutional strengthening across coastal and northeastern WSPs, while project management and technical assistance arrangements continue to provide effective oversight and coordination across all components.", "url": "https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099063026170036715/pdf/P156634-a6497888-50b9-45b4-88d2-1303b0327745.pdf#page=2", "published": "2026-06-30", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05": {"id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.tools.research_evidence:P156634@2026-11-05", "public_from": "2026-11-05", "source": {"tool": "research_evidence", "sources": ["documents", "notices", "projects"]}, "items_total": 60, "counts_by_kind": {"agency_statement": 28, "bank_report": 7, "news": 14, "procurement": 7, "satellite": 1, "site_condition": 3}, "closing_date": "2026-10-31", "late_award_days": 110, "awards_in_window": 6, "direct_awards_in_window": 2, "last_award_date": "2026-10-13", "days_from_last_award_to_closing": 18, "suppliers_checked": null, "suppliers_sanctioned": null, "sources_reached": [{"key": "isr_narrative", "label": "World Bank project reports", "kind": "bank_report", "status": "read", "note": "Prism read 10 passages of the 2 newest ISRs, of June 30, 2026 and March 12, 2026.", "items": 7}, {"key": "procurement_notices", "label": "World Bank procurement notices", "kind": "procurement", "status": "read", "note": "Prism read 7 contract awards and invitations for bids published from May 9, 2026 to November 5, 2026.", "items": 7}, {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "status": "200", "note": "Prism read 7 of 8 feeds and kept 0 entries that name the project, its agencies or its places. The Star's feed address answered a web page, not a feed. Of the pages the sample cites, Eastleigh Voice's page answered a bot check page, so the headline stands in for the article.", "items": 14}, {"key": "inspection_panel", "label": "World Bank Inspection Panel", "kind": "complaint", "status": "200", "note": "The Inspection Panel's register lists 197 cases, 0 of them naming P156634.", "items": 0}, {"key": "sentinel2_mosaic", "label": "Sentinel-2 cloudless mosaics", "kind": "satellite", "status": "read", "note": "Prism built the tile addresses of the 2018 and 2025 mosaics for 18 locations and fetched no tile.", "items": 1}, {"key": "mastodon", "label": "Mastodon", "kind": "social", "status": "200", "note": "Prism read 19 tag timelines and kept 5 posts that name a project place or agency together with a sector word.", "items": 0}, {"key": "site_conditions", "label": "GDACS disaster alerts", "kind": "site_condition", "status": "200", "note": "GDACS lists 3 events for Kenya in the window, 3 of them naming it.", "items": 3}, {"key": "agency_statements", "label": "The implementing agencies' own news pages", "kind": "agency_statement", "status": "200", "note": "Prism read the news pages of NWWDA, CWWDA, KWAWASCO and kept 28 entries.", "items": 28}], "sources_not_reached": [{"key": "gdelt_news", "label": "GDELT", "kind": "news", "status": "429", "note": "GDELT limited calls from this network."}, {"key": "sanctions", "label": "World Bank listing of ineligible firms and individuals", "kind": "sanctions", "status": "no list", "note": "The sanctions check did not run: the list needs a download from the page."}]}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/dc25aeb34cfaa835", "public_from": "2022-10-28", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://www.the-star.co.ke/rss/news"}, "kind": "news", "title": "Mombasa residents protest over sewerage renovation payout delays", "text": "At least 270 people have been affected by the multi-million sewage project\n\nRepresentatives of the Old Town community stormed the Mombasa Water Supply and Sanitation Company offices over delayed compensation for residents affected by sewerage line renovation.\n\nThey were accompanied by the Muslims for Human Rights (Muhuri) organisation on Thursday.\n\nLed by Lubnar Omar, the representatives said they wanted their disturbance allowance soon because the renovations had affected their businesses, which led to temporary closures.\n\nAt least 270 people have been affected by the project.\n\n\"The project is good but it disrupted our businesses because of the construction works. In addition, the old pipes spill sewage all over the place,\" Omar said.\n\n\"Muhuri worked with this office (Mombasa Water) to identify and map the affected people in this project,\" Francis Auma, Muhuri's rapid response officer said.\n\n\"It's now more than a month since the listing yet the community members remain confused by the delay in releasing the compensation , which is their right.\"\n\nThe World Bank, through Mowasco, in August started renovations of the water supply and sewerage lines at Old Town to protect and preserve its heritage, a primary tourist attraction site.\n\nOmar said the repairs at Old Town also caused dirt and sewage to spill over to their houses, putting their health at risk.\n\nOn August 23, 2022, Muhuri held a meeting with Mowasco and the representatives of the affected people to mediate over the disputed amount which the company would pay in compensation.\n\nDuring the meeting, the two parties settled on Sh6,000 as the amount that every business affected would get.\n\nIn another meeting held on September 1, the affected people met with the water company and were issued award letters for compensation.\n\nIn the meeting, Muhuri's correspondence shows the company agreed to use water bowsers to sprinkle water twice a day to manage the dust caused by the project.\n\nNone of the items that were decided have been implemented yet, according to the lobby and the representatives of the Old Town community.\n\nAuma and community leaders accused Mowasco of holding back their money illegally and said they would camp at their offices until their issues are resolved.\n\nMark Mwambota, the company's project engineer, tried to calm them down in vain.\n\nThey demanded assurance from the company's managing director Abdirahim Farah, who was not in the office.\n\nHowever, the Mowasco boss later addressed the agitated group via a phone call made through Mwambota.\n\nHe promised that all the 270 affected people would get their money by November 22.", "url": "https://www.the-star.co.ke/news/2022-10-28-mombasa-residents-protest-over-sewerage-renovation-payout-delays", "published": "2022-10-28", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "186298", "name": "Mombasa", "latitude": -4.02, "longitude": 39.66667}, "image_urls": [], "author": "The Star", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/a230f243be70415c": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/a230f243be70415c", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/a230f243be70415c", "public_from": "2026-11-05", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "the feeds listed below"}, "kind": "news", "title": "Mombasa water project sparks outrage in Mwembe Tayari over job promises, disruptions", "text": "Mombasa water project sparks outrage in Mwembe Tayari over job promises, disruptions", "url": "https://eastleighvoice.co.ke/coast/196147/mombasa-water-project-sparks-outrage-in-mwembe-tayari-over-job-promises,-disruptions", "published": null, "date_known": false, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "186298", "name": "Mombasa", "latitude": -4.02, "longitude": 39.66667}, "image_urls": [], "author": "Eastleigh Voice", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/595f89b9956ceda3", "public_from": "2026-01-20", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://big3africa.org/feed/"}, "kind": "news", "title": "Kenya's Coast Water Agency launches 4.6MW solar plant to cut power costs", "text": "The Coast Water Works Development Agency (CWWDA) has joined the country’s clean energy transition after unveiling a 4.6-megawatt solar power plant that will sharply cut electricity costs and stabilise water supply.\n\nThe large-scale renewable energy installation aligns with Kenya’s push to green its critical infrastructure, reducing dependence on grid electricity while demonstrating how solar power can be integrated into essential public services.\n\nAt the heart of the project is the Baricho Water Solarisation Plant in Kilifi County, which will supply clean power to boreholes and high-lift pumps that move water across long distances to Mombasa, Kilifi and Malindi.\n\nFor years, the agency relied almost entirely on grid electricity, a dependence that translated into soaring power bills and frequent supply disruptions.\n\nAccording to Project Coordinator David Kanui, CWWDA’s monthly electricity bill has hovered around Sh60 million, putting sustained pressure on water service providers and consumers alike.\n\nTo break that dependence, the agency partnered with development partners led by the World Bank under the Water and Sanitation Development Project (WSDP), which financed the Sh670 million solar installation.\n\nImplemented by the Ministry of Water, Sanitation and Irrigation, the project integrates solar energy directly into the Baricho water system. Once fully operational, it is expected to cut electricity costs by at least Sh10 million.\n\nUnder Phase One, the solar plant will power all 11 boreholes and the high-lift pumping system supplying Malindi. Future phases will extend solar-powered pumping to Mombasa, further reducing reliance on fossil-fuel-based grid power.\n\nThe project, which began in October 2025, is scheduled for completion by March 31, 2026.\n\nIn Malindi, engineers have reported steady progress, with Phase One now 60 percent complete. Solar Engineer Serah Mbwaya said the first phase is expected to be commissioned by the end of this month.\n\nThe agency is also preparing to take advantage of net metering, which would allow excess solar power generated during the day to be fed into the national grid and drawn back at night, reducing the need for large battery systems.", "url": "https://big3africa.org/2026/01/20/kenyas-coast-water-agency-launches-4-6mw-solar-plant-to-cut-power-costs/", "published": "2026-01-20", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "200577", "name": "Baricho Sub-Location", "latitude": -3.1, "longitude": 39.78333}, "image_urls": [], "author": "Big3Africa", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/1daaf047c22f24eb", "public_from": "2026-01-14", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://www.the-star.co.ke/rss/news"}, "kind": "news", "title": "Solar water project set to cut power costs, improve supply in Kilifi", "text": "The first phase of Baricho solar plant is scheduled for completion by April\n\nResidents of Kilifi County are set to benefit from more reliable water supply and reduced water bills following the near completion of a major solar-powered water project at Baricho.\n\nThe project, being implemented by the Coast Water Works Development Agency (CWWDA) with support from the World Bank, is expected to significantly lower electricity costs and stabilise water supply in the region.\n\nThe Baricho water solarisation plant, whose first phase is scheduled for completion by April, will serve residents of Kilifi County before being expanded to cover Mombasa County under a second phase.\n\nOnce fully implemented, the project is expected to address long-standing challenges of power interruptions and high operational costs that have affected water supply.\n\nSpeaking to journalists, Engineer David Kanui, the water project coordinator at the Coast Water Works Development Agency, said the agency has for years struggled with high electricity bills charged by Kenya Power due to the energy-intensive nature of water pumping.\n\n“Water is pumped from the boreholes and then it is pumped again to consumers in Mombasa and Kilifi counties. Because of that, electricity bills have been very high,” Kanui stated.\n\nAccording to Kanui, the agency currently pays about Sh60 million per month in electricity costs, a burden that has often disrupted operations.\n\n“We have had problems when water supply is disrupted because of power being disconnected due to unpaid bills,” he said.\n\nTo address the challenge, Kanui said the World Bank agreed to finance the solar power plant, which has a capacity of 4.6 megawatts, to reduce electricity costs and ensure an uninterrupted water supply.\n\n“The plant will power all the 11 bores and water to Malindi in phase one. Phase two will come in the near future and will power water to Mombasa,” he explained.\n\nHe added that once the project is completed, the agency expects to save at least Sh10 million per month in power bills, savings that will benefit both the water utility and consumers.\n\n“When phase two is done, the bill will reduce by 30 per cent,” Kanui noted.\n\nHe further explained that the system will combine solar energy and grid power, with solar being used during the day and Kenya Power at night and early morning hours.\n\n“During the day, we pump using solar, and then at night and early morning hours, we run on Kenya Power—that is 16 hours with eight hours on solar. We will not stop there, so we are looking at new technologies so that we can go off-grid by installing a battery system,” he said.\n\nThe project, which started in October 2025, is expected to be completed by mid-April at a cost of Sh670 million.\n\nSolar engineer Serah Mbwaya said the project has so far achieved 60 per cent completion, with commissioning of the initial phase expected soon.\n\n“By the end of January we will commission the first phase of phase one,” she said.\n\nMbwaya noted that the primary goal is to lower operational costs and improve sustainability for the water agency.\n\n“Once the work is done, the water agency will be profitable and more sustainable. Going green will also mean we are taking care of the environment,” she said.\n\nShe added that the solar components have a lifespan of 25 years, while the lithium battery systems being installed are expected to last between 10 and 15 years.\n\n“The battery bank helps to even out and stabilise power. If there is a passing cloud, other components can continue to work. It gives a signal to the system so that during the day they are able to harvest as much energy as possible,” Mbwaya explained.\n\nThe agency is now seeking additional partners and donors to support phase two of the project, which will extend solar-powered water pumping to Mombasa County, further reducing costs and improving water reliability across the coastal region.", "url": "https://www.the-star.co.ke/counties/coast/2026-01-14-solar-water-project-set-to-cut-power-costs-improve-supply-in-kilifi", "published": "2026-01-14", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "192064", "name": "Kilifi", "latitude": -3.563, "longitude": 39.644}, "image_urls": [], "author": "The Star", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/df117621dfc94a93", "public_from": "2026-03-13", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://www.standardmedia.co.ke/rss/headlines.php"}, "kind": "news", "title": "Baricho solarisation project promises cheaper water for the Coast", "text": "Water Cabinet Secretary Eric Murithi says the Baricho solarisation project would reduce the cost of pumping water to households in Kilifi County. [File, Standard] Coast residents may soon benefit from a cheaper and reliable water supply as the Baricho solarisation project in Kilifi County nears completion. Water, Sanitation and Irrigation Cabinet Secretary Eric Murithi, said the project, currently at 90 per cent complete, would reduce costs of pumping water to households in the area. The CS spoke during an inspection of the Baricho waterworks in Kilifi County, where the government is installing thousands of solar panels to power water pumping operations. \"We have been dealing with high electricity bills because we are in low-lying areas and need to pump the water,\" Murithi said. \"Currently, the Coast Water Works Development Agency spends between Sh60 million and Sh70 million each month on electricity.\" He explained that water service providers supplying Mombasa, Kwale and Kilifi counties currently cover these electricity costs. Murithi pointed out that the solar project is part of a broader government plan to lower operational costs while expanding water access to millions of coastal residents. \"President William Ruto promised the residents of Kwale, Mombasa, and Kilifi that one day they would have water and that the electricity costs would not be high,\" Murithi said. According to the Cabinet Secretary, a total of 6,716 solar panels have already been installed, and the project is expected to wrap up within the next week. \"We have set up 6,716 panels at the solar farm, which will be finished next week as we are about 90 per cent done. Some systems are already running on solar power,\" he said. Once operational, the solar facility will generate about 4.3 megawatts of power.", "url": "https://www.standardmedia.co.ke/coast/article/2001542940/baricho-solarisation-project-promises-cheaper-water-for-the-coast", "published": "2026-03-13", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "200577", "name": "Baricho Sub-Location", "latitude": -3.1, "longitude": 39.78333}, "image_urls": [], "author": "The Standard", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/e25eedf42e385947", "public_from": "2026-06-04", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://www.dawan.africa/rss.xml"}, "kind": "news", "title": "Wajir set for major sanitation upgrade as historic sludge treatment plant nears completion", "text": "Uncovering the Continent — Through Its Own Lens\n\nGet the latest breaking news and analysis delivered to your inbox.\n\nJoin our community of informed readers. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nKenya, 4 June 2026 - For decades, many residents of Wajir have relied on the traditional bucket toilet system due to the county's unique geographical challenges.\n\nThat era is gradually coming to an end as the government pushes ahead with the construction of a modern Faecal Sludge Treatment Plant (FSTP), a landmark project expected to transform sanitation services in the region.Water and Sanitation Secretary Eng. Samuel Alima on Wednesday inspected the ongoing construction of the facility, located about five kilometres from Wajir town.\n\nThe project, being implemented under the Water and Sanitation Development Program (WSDP) in partnership with Wajir Water and Sewerage Company (WAJIWASCO) and the World Bank, is designed to provide a safe and environmentally sustainable solution for waste management in the county.\n\nAccording to the Ministry of Water, Sanitation and Irrigation, the facility is currently 64% complete.\n\nWajir's flat sandy terrain and high water table have historically made conventional pit latrines unsuitable, as they pose a risk of contaminating underground water sources. The new treatment plant is expected to address these challenges by safely processing human waste while protecting the environment and public health.\n\nDuring the inspection, Eng. Alima urged the contractor and project consultant to accelerate construction works to ensure the facility is completed and commissioned on schedule.\n\nThe project is expected to significantly improve sanitation standards and public health outcomes in Wajir, where residents have depended on bucket-based sanitation systems for generations.\n\nMeanwhile, Principal Secretary for Water and Sanitation Julius Korir and Water Secretary Alima toured several water projects under the Horn of Africa Groundwater for Resilience Program (HoAGW4RP) in Wajir West Constituency.\n\nKAA moves airport tenders online, opening doors for youth and small businesses\n\nKenya expands digital hubs beyond Konza in push to turn youth skills into jobs\n\nThe officials inspected projects in Arbajahan, Adan Awale, Kobeysurur and Griftu, which are aimed at expanding access to clean and safe water in the drought-prone county.\n\nThe ministry said the projects have reached an overall implementation level of 19% and include borehole rehabilitation and solarisation, construction of water transmission and distribution pipelines, development of storage facilities, installation of water kiosks and livestock watering points, and establishment of water treatment facilities.\n\nOnce completed, the projects are expected to provide clean water to more than 63,000 residents and support over 500,000 livestock, boosting water security and livelihoods across the county.\n\nMr Korir called for better coordination of water projects being implemented under different government programmes to avoid duplication and maximise benefits to local communities.\n\nHe also directed project consultants to ensure all villages are connected to the water distribution network.\n\n\"No community should be left behind in accessing clean and safe water,\" the PS said during the inspection.\n\nThe government said the projects form part of broader efforts to strengthen climate resilience and accelerate socio-economic development in Kenya's arid and semi-arid lands through sustainable water infrastructure investments.\n\nExplainer: KRA collects Sh1 trillion, but Kenyans may be paying more\n\nPortuguese Football Federation Thanks Cristiano Ronaldo for 23-Year Service", "url": "https://www.dawan.africa/news/wajir-set-for-major-sanitation-upgrade-as-historic-sludge-treatment-plant-nears-completion", "published": "2026-06-04", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "178440", "name": "Wajir District", "latitude": 1.75, "longitude": 40.01667}, "image_urls": [], "author": "Dawan Africa", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00456907": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00456907", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00456907", "public_from": "2026-07-15", "source": {"key": "procurement_notices", "label": "World Bank procurement notices", "kind": "procurement", "license": "The World Bank: Procurement Notices: Systematic Tracking of Exchanges in Procurement (STEP), from search.worldbank.org/api/v2/procnotices. License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement?projectId=P156634"}, "kind": "procurement", "title": "Contract Award OP00456907, KE-NWWDA-541061-CW-RFQ (Request for Quotations): Equipping of Exploratory Boreholes Project - Lot 2- Nurgows Water Supply", "text": "Contract Award Project: P156634-Water and Sanitation Development Project Loan/Credit/TF Info: IDA-60300 Bid/Contract Reference No: KE-NWWDA-541061-CW-RFQ Procurement Method: RFQ-Request for Quotations Scope of Contract: Equipping of Exploratory Boreholes Project - Lot 2 - Nurgows Water Supply Notice Version No: 0 Date Notification of Award Issued (YYYY/MM/DD) 2026/05/04 Duration of Contract 30 Day(s) Awarded Bidder(s): MASTERWEB AGENCY LIMITED (1105433) Po Box 58141 - 00200 Nairobi Country: Kenya Bid Price at Opening KES 38120662.80 Evaluated Bid Price KES 38120662.80 Signed Contract price KES 38120662.80", "url": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement-detail/OP00456907", "published": "2026-07-15", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank procurement notices (STEP)", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": {"notice_date": "2026-07-15", "award_notification_date": "2026-05-04"}}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474946": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474946", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474946", "public_from": "2026-10-13", "source": {"key": "procurement_notices", "label": "World Bank procurement notices", "kind": "procurement", "license": "The World Bank: Procurement Notices: Systematic Tracking of Exchanges in Procurement (STEP), from search.worldbank.org/api/v2/procnotices. License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement?projectId=P156634"}, "kind": "procurement", "title": "Contract Award OP00474946, KE-NWWDA-541055-CW-RFB (Request for Bids): Equipping of Exploratory Boreholes Project-Lot 1- Sarman - Tarbaj Water Supply", "text": "Contract Award Project: P156634-Water and Sanitation Development Project Loan/Credit/TF Info: IDA-60300 Bid/Contract Reference No: KE-NWWDA-541055-CW-RFB Procurement Method: RFB-Request for Bids Scope of Contract: Equipping of Exploratory Boreholes Projects Lot 1 Sarman Tarbaj Water Supply Project in Wajir County Notice Version No: 0 Date Notification of Award Issued (YYYY/MM/DD) 2026/06/22 Duration of Contract 90 Day(s) Awarded Bidder(s): OSB AFRICA LIMITED (1135040) P.O Box 23001-00505Nairobi Country: Kenya Bid Price at Opening KES Evaluated Bid Price KES 166668894.48 Signed Contract price KES 166668894.48", "url": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement-detail/OP00474946", "published": "2026-10-13", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank procurement notices (STEP)", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": {"notice_date": "2026-10-13", "award_notification_date": "2026-06-22"}}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/24d1ca8a593f492b", "public_from": "2022-05-16", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://www.the-star.co.ke/rss/news"}, "kind": "news", "title": "Sh280m Kipevu wastewater treatment plant to be ready by October", "text": "The project is expected to serve more than 17,000 Mombasa residents\n\nThe Kipevu Wastewater Treatment and Re-pooling Plant in Mombasa is being rehabilitated at Sh280 million.\n\nWater Chief Administrative Secretary Andrew Tuimur said once complete, the project is expected to serve more than 17,000 Kipevu residents.\n\n“The project, which will also serve adjacent areas through exhausters is going well and is expected to be done by October this year,” he said.\n\nTuimur said the old Kipevu treatment plant, which was constructed in 1980 is unable to serve the increased population.\n\nSpeaking in Mombasa over the weekend, the CAS said sanitation has been a big issue, not only in Mombasa but in the entire country.\n\n“We are a bit behind on the issue of sanitation, overall in the country we are at 26 per cent,” he said.\n\nTuimur said the Water ministry will speed up construction works for the water distribution network in Mombasa to ensure all the projects are completed before October.\n\nHe said the ministry had aligned all its programmes to have an element of sanitation as they work towards improving water distribution networks.\n\n“Every programme we are starting now emphasises that it must have a sanitation element. As we produce more water, we also improve on the sanitation programme,” the CAS said.\n\nTuimur also inspected ongoing water distribution networks along Mombasa North mainland areas of Nyali and Kisauni.\n\nThe Kisauni distribution network stretches about 140km and is being constructed at Sh700 million.\n\nThe project, expected to be completed by October this year, will serve more than 200,000 people living in the northern mainland of Mombasa.\n\nTuimur inspected the ongoing works of the 65km distribution network in Nyali being constructed at a cost of Sh516 million.\n\n“The project progress is at 36.54 per cent, we expect it will be done by October. It will serve around 216,577 people,” Tuimur said.\n\nThe 18-month contract was awarded to Danaff Kenya Company Limited last year. Construction work started on May 1.\n\nSo far Sh198,801,856 has been paid to the contractor.\n\nTuimur said the country’s water coverage was 65 per cent.\n\n“We would like that to reach 100 per cent before 2030. That is why we are visiting these projects to ensure they are completed on time,” he said.\n\nThe CAS said the Water ministry has 685 projects spread across the country ranging from small, medium to large scale.\n\nHe said the distribution networks being done were in preparation for when the Sh20 billion Mwache Multipurpose Dam project will be completed.\n\n“We want to be ready for the water that will come from Mwache by ensuring distribution channels are in place,” Tuimur said.\n\nHe said the contractor is on site and would begin the work soon.\n\nThe construction of the dam, whose contract was signed in 2018, was set to begin on February 15 this year, taking a period of seven years.\n\nThe project was delayed for several years as contractor Syno Hydro awaited the government to finalise compensation of land.\n\nApproximately 12,000 people will be displaced by the project which requires 250,000 hectares (617,763 acres) of land.\n\nThe project is funded by the World Bank through the Kenya Water Security Programme.\n\n“We had problems with way-leaves and compensation which we have already sorted out, work will begin soon,” Tuimur said.", "url": "https://www.the-star.co.ke/counties/coast/2022-05-16-sh280m-kipevu-wastewater-treatment-plant-to-be-ready-by-october/", "published": "2022-05-16", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "186298", "name": "Mombasa", "latitude": -4.02, "longitude": 39.66667}, "image_urls": [], "author": "The Star", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p6-s7", "public_from": "2026-03-12", "source": {"key": "isr_narrative", "label": "World Bank project reports", "kind": "bank_report", "license": "The World Bank: Documents & Reports (documents.worldbank.org). License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentlist?projid=P156634&docty_exact=Implementation+Status+and+Results+Report"}, "kind": "bank_report", "title": "ISR ISR07091, March 12, 2026: Results indicators, comments on achieving targets, page 6", "text": "(Number) achieving targets Additional Water delivered 39.30 Apr/2017 44.50 30-Jan-2026 44.50 30-Jan-2026 44.50 Oct/2026 by WSPs to customers due to Comments on Targets expected to be exceeded once Kwale contracts are complete project interventions (as achieving targets reflected in water bills) (Cubic Meter(m3)) People provided with access 0.00 Apr/2017 5,100.00 30-Jan-2026 5,100.00 30-Jan-2026 4,800.00 Oct/2026 to improved shared Comments on Almost all planned ablution blocks are complete or nearing completion. sanitation facilities under the achieving targets project. (Number) Volume of biological oxygen 0.00 Apr/2017 0.00 30-Jan-2026 0.00 30-Jan-2026 1,600.00 Oct/2026 demand pollution loads Comments on Rehabilitation of Kipevu ongoing removed by treatment plants achieving targets supported under the project. (Tones/year) Hours of service per day 10.00 Apr/2017 17.00 30-Jan-2026 17.00 30-Jan-2026 12.00 Oct/2026 provided by coastal WSPs Comments on Target exceeded benefiting from the project. achieving targets (Hours) Non-revenue water (% of 50.00 Apr/2017 43.00 30-Jan-2026 43.00 30-Jan-2026 43.00 Oct/2026 water that a service provider supplies to its distribution system that is not reflected in revenues). (Percentage) Operations and maintenance 90.00 Apr/2017 83.00 30-Jan-2026 83.00 30-Jan-2026 100.00 Oct/2026 costs of coastal WSPs Comments on This is from the previous IMPACT report. The number will be updated with the next IMPACT benefiting from the project achieving targets report recovered from customers. (Percentage) Septic sludge treatment 0.00 Apr/2017 0.00 30-Jan-2026 0.00 30-Jan-2026 4.00 Oct/2026 facilities constructed under Comments on Construction ongoing. Expected to be complete by August 2026 the project (Number) achieving targets Component 3: National performance-based financing (US$83.6 million of which IDA-SUF US$76 million).", "url": "https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099031226003019207/pdf/P156634-4691aab6-8be2-47be-a151-186b5d20a1b3.pdf#page=6", "published": "2026-03-12", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474700": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474700", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474700", "public_from": "2026-10-12", "source": {"key": "procurement_notices", "label": "World Bank procurement notices", "kind": "procurement", "license": "The World Bank: Procurement Notices: Systematic Tracking of Exchanges in Procurement (STEP), from search.worldbank.org/api/v2/procnotices. License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement?projectId=P156634"}, "kind": "procurement", "title": "Invitation for Bids OP00474700, KE-NWWDA-541055-CW-RFB (Request for Bids): Equipping of Exploratory Boreholes Project-Lot 1- Sarman - Tarbaj Water Supply", "text": "REQUEST FOR BIDS (RFB) Country: Kenya Project: WATER AND SANITATION DEVELOPMENT PROJECT Credit No.: IDA-60300 Ref: KE-NWWDA-541055-CW-RFB RFB Title: Equipping of Exploratory Boreholes Project-Lot 1- Sarman - Tarbaj Water Supply County Contract No. KE. KE-NWWDA-541055-CW-RFB The Northern Water Works Development Agency has received financing from the World Bank toward the cost of the Water and Sanitation Development Project , and intends to apply part of the proceeds toward payments under the contract for Equipping of Exploratory Boreholes Project-Lot 1- Sarman - Tarbaj Water Supply , Contract No. KE-NWWDA-541055-CW-RFB. The Northern Water Works Development Agency now invites sealed bids from eligible bidders for Equipping of Exploratory Boreholes Project-Lot 1- Sarman - Tarbaj Water Supply , Contract No. KE-NWWDA-541055-CW-RFB , as detailed below:, , 160/140 OD, PN10-16, and associated civil works Borehole Equipping with Solar. HDPE Rising Main 40KM , 160/140 OD, PN10-16, and associated civil works Bidding will be conducted through the National Competitive Bidding procurement using a Request for Bids as specified in the World Bank’s “Procurement Regulations” for IPF Borrower and is open to all eligible Bidders as defined in the procurement Regulations. Interested eligible Bidders may obtain further information from Northern Water Works Development Agency, Procurement office (A3 Ground floor), email [contact removed] and inspect the bidding documents during office hours 00900 to 1600 hours at the address given below The bidding document in English may be downloaded free of charge from our website www.nwwda.go.ke or www.tenders.go.ke - Public Procurement Information Portal Addendum/Clarifications will also be posted in the NWWDA website immediately they become available Bids must be delivered to the address below on or before the 12th May 2026 at 11.00 am. Electronic bidding will not be permitted. Late bids will be rejected. Bids will be publicly opened in the presence of the bidders’ designated representatives and anyone who choose to attend at the address below at Conference Room, at 11.30 am on 12th May 2026. All Bids shall be accompanied by a Bid Security (Unconditional Bank guarantee) of Kshs. 2,000,000.00 (Two Million only) The address referred to above is: Chief Executive Officer Northern Water Works Development Agency Maji House, Kismayu Road P.O. Box 495 – 70100 GARISSA, KENYA Fax: [contact removed] [contact removed] Website: www.nwwda.go.ke", "url": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement-detail/OP00474700", "published": "2026-10-12", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank procurement notices (STEP)", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": {"notice_date": "2026-10-12", "submission_date": "2026-10-12"}}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474696": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474696", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00474696", "public_from": "2026-10-12", "source": {"key": "procurement_notices", "label": "World Bank procurement notices", "kind": "procurement", "license": "The World Bank: Procurement Notices: Systematic Tracking of Exchanges in Procurement (STEP), from search.worldbank.org/api/v2/procnotices. License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement?projectId=P156634"}, "kind": "procurement", "title": "Contract Award OP00474696, KE-NWWDA-541062-CW-RFQ (Request for Quotations): Equipping of Exploratory Boreholes project- Lot 3- Qaras Water Supply", "text": "Contract Award Project: P156634-Water and Sanitation Development Project Loan/Credit/TF Info: IDA-60300 Bid/Contract Reference No: KE-NWWDA-541062-CW-RFQ Procurement Method: RFQ-Request for Quotations Scope of Contract: Borehole Equipping with Solar , Rising Main 4.1Km, HDPE PN 10, OD 125mm Distribution mains of 63mm- 110 PN8 OD,3.2 KM, Elevated Steel Tank 100m3 and associated Civil Works Notice Version No: 0 Date Notification of Award Issued (YYYY/MM/DD) 2026/05/04 Duration of Contract 90 Day(s) Awarded Bidder(s): YATICO SUPPLIES AND CONSTRUCTION LIMITED (1134841) Po Box 488-70100 Garissa Country: Kenya Bid Price at Opening KES 38896151.80 Evaluated Bid Price KES 38896151.80 Signed Contract price KES 38896151.80", "url": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement-detail/OP00474696", "published": "2026-10-12", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank procurement notices (STEP)", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": {"notice_date": "2026-10-12", "award_notification_date": "2026-05-04"}}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/b87138d428e8a344": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/b87138d428e8a344", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/b87138d428e8a344", "public_from": "2026-10-08", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://www.kenyanews.go.ke/feed/"}, "kind": "news", "title": "Lobby demands solution to Changamwe-Jomvu water crisis", "text": "A civil society organisation is demanding urgent intervention by both national and county government leaders over what it describes as a prolonged water crisis affecting residents of Changamwe, Jomvu sub counties and the wider Mombasa County, warning it may invoke constitutional rights to protest if the situation is not resolved.\n\nSalim Suleiman, Executive Director of the Haki na Usawa Organisation, said the group had taken note of public notices issued by the Mombasa Water Supply and Sanitation Company Limited (MOWASCO), dated 12th and 18th September 2026, attributing supply interruptions in the West Mainland region, including Jomvu and Changamwe sub-counties, to a leakage along the Mzima pipeline at Tsavo West National Park.\n\n“In our view, this notice is just a public relations exercise. The reality on the ground is that there has been no water even before the notice,” Suleiman said, adding that residents across Changamwe, Jomvu and nearly the entire county have gone months without clean and safe water, forcing households to rely on expensive and unsafe vendor water.\n\nHe said the burden falls heaviest on women, children and low-income families, describing the situation, as “a total failure” by MOWASCO.\n\nCiting Article 43(1) of the Constitution of Kenya, which guarantees every person the right to clean and safe water, Suleiman said the organisation’s demands include immediate intervention by Mombasa elected national and county leaders to pursue the matter, the disbandment or resignation of the Coast Water Board over its failure to execute its mandate, and the immediate resumption of water services.\n\nFailure to heed the demands, the organisation said it would exercise its rights under Article 37 of the Constitution, which guarantees the right to picket and petition.\n\nMary Taura, a resident of the Chaani area, said the prevailing lack of clean water has forced many households to buy water from vendors at between Sh25 and Sh30, when available, and described the hardship of having to rely on salty water in its absence.\n\n“We can’t even drink clean water; it is difficult to drink salt water,” she said, appealing for help for affected residents.\n\nGeorge Ondara, a resident representative from the Changamwe area, said an earlier public participation forum with a water engineer had promised a quick resolution, but called the MOWASCO notice “just a PR” gesture.\n\nHe said water supply remains inconsistent across different parts of Changamwe and Jomvu, with some areas such as Kipevu receiving water, while others go without for extended periods, and called for a clear, published supply schedule so residents know when to expect water.\n\nOndara also raised concerns over possible corruption within MOWASCO, alleging that some unauthorized vendors appear to have preferential access to water supply and called for an internal investigation into the utility’s staff.\n\nMzee Oksimba, a representative for Port Reitz Ward, appealed directly to the Mombasa Governor to urgently address the crisis, reminding him that affected residents are voters.\n\nHe said residents remain vulnerable without clean water. He noted that concerns over water cartels have persisted across different county administrations and called for closer collaboration between national and county governments to address the issue, which he said continues to deny ordinary residents reliable access to water despite repeated promises.", "url": "https://www.kenyanews.go.ke/lobby-demands-solution-to-changamwe-jomvu-water-crisis/", "published": "2026-10-08", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "186298", "name": "Mombasa", "latitude": -4.02, "longitude": 39.66667}, "image_urls": [], "author": "Kenya News Agency", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00471125", "public_from": "2026-09-25", "source": {"key": "procurement_notices", "label": "World Bank procurement notices", "kind": "procurement", "license": "The World Bank: Procurement Notices: Systematic Tracking of Exchanges in Procurement (STEP), from search.worldbank.org/api/v2/procnotices. License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement?projectId=P156634"}, "kind": "procurement", "title": "Contract Award OP00471125, KE-CWSB-567630-CW-DIR (Direct Selection): Solar System for the Ukunda Water Supply", "text": "Contract Award Project: P156634-Water and Sanitation Development Project Loan/Credit/TF Info: IDA-60290 Bid/Contract Reference No: KE-CWSB-567630-CW-DIR Procurement Method: DIR-Direct Selection Scope of Contract: Construction of Solar System for the Ukunda Water Supply Notice Version No: 0 Date Notification of Award Issued (YYYY/MM/DD) 2026/08/28 Duration of Contract 425 Day(s) Awarded Bidder(s): CHINA INTERNATIONAL WATER & ELECTRIC CORP. (CWE) (1079683) P.O BOX 38653 NAIROBI, KENYA Country: China Bid Price at Opening KES 314838645.42 Evaluated Bid Price KES 314838645.42 Signed Contract price KES 314838645.42", "url": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement-detail/OP00471125", "published": "2026-09-25", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank procurement notices (STEP)", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": {"notice_date": "2026-09-25", "award_notification_date": "2026-08-28"}}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00466974": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00466974", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00466974", "public_from": "2026-09-04", "source": {"key": "procurement_notices", "label": "World Bank procurement notices", "kind": "procurement", "license": "The World Bank: Procurement Notices: Systematic Tracking of Exchanges in Procurement (STEP), from search.worldbank.org/api/v2/procnotices. License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement?projectId=P156634"}, "kind": "procurement", "title": "Contract Award OP00466974, KE-CWSB-565568-GO-RFQ (Request for Quotations): Supply and installation of Additional Pumps at Baricho", "text": "Contract Award Project: P156634-Water and Sanitation Development Project Loan/Credit/TF Info: IDA-60290 Bid/Contract Reference No: KE-CWSB-565568-GO-RFQ Procurement Method: RFQ-Request for Quotations Scope of Contract: SUPPLY AND INSTALLATION OF ADDITIONAL PUMPS AT BARICHO Notice Version No: 0 Date Notification of Award Issued (YYYY/MM/DD) 2026/08/31 Duration of Contract 2 Month(s) Awarded Bidder(s): CHINA INTERNATIONAL WATER & ELECTRIC CORP. (CWE) (1079683) P.O BOX 38653 NAIROBI, KENYA Country: China Bid Price at Opening KES 14508236.00 Evaluated Bid Price KES 14508236.00 Signed Contract price KES 14508236.00 Rejected Bidder(s): STANDARD TECHNICAL SERVICE &GENERAL SUPPLIES LTD (447462) 81688 - 80100MOMBASA Country: Kenya Bid Price at Opening KES 28633788.00 Evaluated Bid Price KES Reason for Rejection Non Responsive TAIFENG UNITED (KENYA) M&E ENGINEERING COMPANY (1113436) [contact removed] Country: Kenya Bid Price at Opening KES 15273198.00 Evaluated Bid Price KES Reason for Rejection Non Responsive", "url": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement-detail/OP00466974", "published": "2026-09-04", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank procurement notices (STEP)", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": {"notice_date": "2026-09-04", "award_notification_date": "2026-08-31"}}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00457686": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00457686", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/procurement_notices/OP00457686", "public_from": "2026-07-20", "source": {"key": "procurement_notices", "label": "World Bank procurement notices", "kind": "procurement", "license": "The World Bank: Procurement Notices: Systematic Tracking of Exchanges in Procurement (STEP), from search.worldbank.org/api/v2/procnotices. License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement?projectId=P156634"}, "kind": "procurement", "title": "Contract Award OP00457686, KE-TAVEVO-528767-CW-DIR (Direct Selection): Proposed Construction of Vindo Booster Station Works", "text": "Contract Award Project: P156634-Water and Sanitation Development Project Loan/Credit/TF Info: IDA-60300 Bid/Contract Reference No: KE-TAVEVO-528767-CW-DIR Procurement Method: DIR-Direct Selection Scope of Contract: PROPOSED CONSTRUCTION OF THE VINDO BOOSTER STATION WORKS Notice Version No: 0 Date Notification of Award Issued (YYYY/MM/DD) 2026/04/10 Duration of Contract 3 Month(s) Awarded Bidder(s): CHINA INTERNATIONAL WATER & ELECTRIC CORP (1105950) P.O BOX 38653-00100NAIROBI, KENYA Country: China Bid Price at Opening KES 61710496.44 Evaluated Bid Price KES 61710496.44 Signed Contract price KES 61710496.44", "url": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/procurement-detail/OP00457686", "published": "2026-07-20", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank procurement notices (STEP)", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": {"notice_date": "2026-07-20", "award_notification_date": "2026-04-10"}}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/record-P513697": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/record-P513697", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/record-P513697", "public_from": "2026-03-20", "source": {"key": "isr_narrative", "label": "World Bank project reports", "kind": "bank_report", "license": "The World Bank: Documents & Reports (documents.worldbank.org). License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://search.worldbank.org/api/v3/projects?format=json&id=P513697&fl=*"}, "kind": "bank_report", "title": "World Bank project record: Coastal Region Water Security and Climate Resilience Project (P513697)", "text": "Coastal Region Water Security and Climate Resilience Project (P513697).\n\nBoard approval date: 2026-03-20.\n\nStatus: Active.\n\nProject development objective: The development objective of this project (KWSCRP-2) is to increase bulk water supply to Mombasa County and Kwale County, and increase access to water in Kwale County.", "url": "https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/project-detail/P513697", "published": "2026-03-20", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p2-s4": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p2-s4", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/isr_narrative/40091292-p2-s4", "public_from": "2026-03-12", "source": {"key": "isr_narrative", "label": "World Bank project reports", "kind": "bank_report", "license": "The World Bank: Documents & Reports (documents.worldbank.org). License: CC BY 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentlist?projid=P156634&docty_exact=Implementation+Status+and+Results+Report"}, "kind": "bank_report", "title": "ISR ISR07091, March 12, 2026: Implementation Status and Key Decisions, page 2", "text": "The project has made significant progress in the completion of critical contracts needed to attain the project’s development objective. Overall physical progress of works contracts is at 85 percent, with 46 percent of works contracts and all goods contracts completed. Several of these completed works are already benefiting the intended beneficiaries including water distributions works in all seven WSPs in the Project, water kiosks in Garissa, ablution blocks in Ukunda, Kwale and Wajir towns, sewer connections in Mombasa and the Baricho bulk water supply scheme whose capacity has been expanded to serve Mombasa and Kilifi counties better and its resilience to flooding risks enhanced. The remaining works contracts are expected to be completed within the remaining project period. Grievances raised under the project are being closely monitored by both the Bank and the Ministry for their resolution ahead of the project closure.", "url": "https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099031226003019207/pdf/P156634-4691aab6-8be2-47be-a151-186b5d20a1b3.pdf#page=2", "published": "2026-03-12", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": null, "image_urls": [], "author": "World Bank", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/4f236e25b63dcc1f", "public_from": "2025-05-16", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://www.kenyanews.go.ke/feed/"}, "kind": "news", "title": "CS Mugaa launches strategic plan to improve water access for six counties", "text": "The Cabinet Secretary for Water, Sanitation and Irrigation Eng. Eric Mugaa has launched an ambitious 5-year strategic plan for the Northern Water Works Development Agency to improve access to water and sanitation in six counties.\n\nThe plan for 2024 – 2028, which will require funding of approximately Sh63.7 billion in the next 5 years to be fully implemented, seeks to improve access to water in urban areas from 34 per cent to 44 per cent and 22 per cent to 32 per cent in rural areas.\n\nIn addition, the Agency targets to improve access to sanitation from 8 percent to 13 percent in urban areas and 29.1 percent to 34.1 percent in rural areas.\n\nTo achieve the goals, the agency will construct 346 water pans as a drought resilience measure and construct the Rahole Canal as a flood control measure from River Tana to hinterland.\n\nAt least 400 boreholes of which 100 will be solarized and six others to be powered by wind energy are set to be drilled across the six counties of Garissa, Wajir, Mandera, Marsabit, Isiolo, and Samburu where the NWWDA operates.\n\nAdditionally, 400 boreholes will be drilled or rehabilitated out of which 100 will be solarized while six others will run on wind power.\n\nAt the same time, 15,000 metres of roof catchment gutters will be installed, and connected to 250 water tanks of five cubic metres, and 100 sanitation facilities developed in public institutions across the region.\n\nSpeaking during the launch, the CS called on the water agencies to work closely with county governments and other development partners in order to realize the broader plan.\n\n“I ask the board and the CEO to work hand in hand with the county governments because for us to achieve the target of Sh63 billion we need to pull in the same direction. We also need to work with NGOs and attract blended financing through public private partnerships and that is why we amended the Water Act 2016, to allow the agencies to become bulk water suppliers,” Mugaa said.\n\n“The Ministry has invested more than Sh12 billion in this region but the water connectivity on the ground tells a different story. Irrespective of the write-ups we do, if the citizens have no access to water then they are all in vain,” he added.\n\nPrincipal Secretary for Water Julius Korir called for the stakeholders to invest in the more sustainable projects which will have a continuous impact on the citizens.\n\n“This region has been receiving a lot of money in the last few years especially funds addressing water supply challenges and this is an area we need to relook at our strategies. There is an audit report that came from the national audit office looking at the water infrastructure in the region and the verdict is that we have invested in small water pans that end up not being sustainable,” Korir said.\n\n“As we roll out this strategic plan today, we need to look at this aspect so that we can invest in more sustainable projects,” he added.", "url": "https://www.kenyanews.go.ke/?p=150106", "published": "2025-05-16", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "197744", "name": "Garissa District", "latitude": -0.172, "longitude": 40.041}, "image_urls": [], "author": "Kenya News Agency", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/c1f6a8f12b71bd4a": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/c1f6a8f12b71bd4a", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/c1f6a8f12b71bd4a", "public_from": "2025-01-25", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://www.standardmedia.co.ke/rss/headlines.php"}, "kind": "news", "title": "Governor Mwadime calls for winding up of water agency", "text": "When Taita Taveta Governor Andrew Mwadime appeared before the CPAC committee at the Bunge Towers on June 3, 2024. [Elvis Ogina, Standard] A row has erupted between the Taita Taveta County government and a water agency over the provision of water in a region that has largely remained water insecure. Today Governor Andrew Mwadime stated that he was pushing for the disbandment of the Coast Water Works Development Agency for failing to supply water to the region. He instead called for the establishment of county water firms with the responsibility to provide water across the six coastal counties. Addressing ranchers at the Voi Wildlife Lodge, Mwadime argued that local water firms would be better suited to deliver water efficiently, claiming that the water agency had 'outlived' its usefulness. \"The Coast Water Works Agency is only interested in water and money at the expense of service delivery. The state agency should be disbanded and counties given the right to establish their own water firms,\" he said. However, the agency quickly hit back, blaming the county government for the water challenges facing the region. Director Judith Wabosha stated that the agency was working directly with communities to manage water projects. Wabosha, who last week accompanied agency chairperson Dr Daniel Mwaringa in commissioning two water projects in the Wundanyi sub-county, said many boreholes had fallen into disuse after the county failed to maintain them. \"We have several boreholes that are not working because the county government failed to maintain them, in addition to not settling water and electricity bills,\" she told The Standard. Wabosha asserted that the agency had made significant efforts and should not be blamed for the region's water problems. \"We are working directly with the local community after the county administration failed to revive stalled boreholes constructed by the agency. \"We are working with water committees elected by the local residents to implement water projects,\" she added.", "url": "https://thestandard.ke/business/coast/article/2001510389/governor-mwadime-calls-for-winding-up-of-water-agency", "published": "2025-01-25", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "7667652", "name": "Taita Taveta", "latitude": -3.4, "longitude": 38.37}, "image_urls": [], "author": "The Standard", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/57eebce979ca2f45", "public_from": "2024-11-21", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://peopledaily.digital/feed"}, "kind": "news", "title": "Coast water agency takes most incompetent institution tag in sector", "text": "The Coast Water Works Development Agency has been named as the most incompetent agency in the water sector with almost all the projects delayed in implementation, a report by the Auditor General has revealed.\n\nThe taxpayer has in the process lost with the target population continuing to suffer water shortage as a result, the auditor report for the year 2022/20223 states.\n\nThe delayed projects include the construction of Baricho Well-Field Protection Works which had been allocated Sh 3.6 billion in 2021 but work is still in progress.\n\n“The statement of receipts and payments reflects an amount of Sh3.4 billion for the acquisition of non-financial assets which includes Sh3,296,374,116 for construction of civil works. Included in this amount is Sh791,253,843 relating to the construction of Baricho well-field protection works,” states the auditor.\n\nThe report further states that the Agency entered into a contract on November 17, 2021 executable within twelve months, from November 17, 2021, to November 16, 2022. As of the time of audit inspection in the month of October 2023, the project had not been completed and the defect liability period of 12 months had already commenced.\n\nIn yet another project, the Agency signed a contract in respect of the program for the improvement of water services in Mombasa County and construction works for North Mainland (Nyali) phase 1 at a contract sum of Sh516,433,325.\n\nThe contract sum was revised to Sh589,861,167 during the financial year 2019/2020. The project had an expected completion period of 18 months to October 25, 2022. The completion date was later extended to August 31, 2023.\n\nFurther, on August 31, 2023, the contractor was issued with a notice for an intention to charge liquidated damages if the contractor did not improve or accelerate the implementation of the outstanding works as of September 1, 2023.\n\n“As at June 2023, the contractor had been paid a total of Sh 370.9 million through eight (8) certificates; an equivalent of 63% of the contract price. As at the time of the audit in October, 2023, the contractor had no extension of time and the performance security of Sh58,986,116 had expired on 22 October 2023,” reads the report.\n\nThe construction of the Mombasa North Mainland Short-Term Water Distribution System, a key water project for the region has stalled for three years and no explanation was given by the management.\n\nThe report states that the agency entered into a contract on 23rd March 2021 in respect of the construction of the Mombasa North Mainland short-term water distribution system at a contract sum of Sh516.4 million but the figure was later revised upwards to Sh781. 4 million.\n\nThe project was expected to be completed within a period of 18 months to October 25, 2022. The completion date was later extended to October 7, 2023.\n\n“The project completion status was at 68% at the expiry of the extended project completion date and in the circumstances, the project delay may result in escalated costs for completion, and value for money may not be obtained,” notes the auditor.", "url": "https://peopledaily.digital/news/coast-water-agency-takes-most-incompetent-institution-tag-in-sector", "published": "2024-11-21", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "200577", "name": "Baricho Sub-Location", "latitude": -3.1, "longitude": 39.78333}, "image_urls": [], "author": "People Daily", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/outlet_feeds/79bb66e7e1850e49", "public_from": "2024-06-15", "source": {"key": "outlet_feeds", "label": "News outlets' feeds", "kind": "news", "license": "Each article belongs to its outlet; Prism keeps its words with a link to the article, for reference.", "url_of_source": "https://www.the-star.co.ke/rss/news"}, "kind": "news", "title": "State completes Sh4 billion water works in Kilifi", "text": "The project seeks to address perennial water problem in Kilifi and Mombasa counties.\n\nThe government has completed Sh4 billion water project in Baricho in Kilifi county.\n\nIt includes rehabilitation of three boreholes destroyed by floods in 2018 when Sabaki River burst its banks.\n\nThe construction cost the government Sh1.4 billion, including the Baricho wellfield protection project to protect the wells from future floods.\n\nThe Baricho-Kakuyuni high-volume water pipeline was done for Sh2 billion and is currently in use.\n\nWater and Sanitation CS Zacharia Njeru toured the project on Thursday with Malindi MP Amina Mnyazi, Coast Water Works Development Agency officials.\n\nThey said the project is ready for commissioning by President William Ruto.\n\nThe pumping capacity at the water wells was 40 million litres of water per day.\n\n“As a government, we are aware that this project will be of great help to Coast residents. We know there is water shortage and that’s why we want to set up a date for its official opening,” Njeru said.\n\nHe said the project will transform people's lives by providing water round the clock.\n\nNjeru said the government is working on plans to install solar power panels at the site to reduce pumping costs.\n\n\"To address the high cost of electricity, which amounts to more than Sh60 million, I direct direct the Coast Water Works CEO to conduct feasibility studies in the next three months to come up with a solar power-driven pumping system,\" he said.\n\nThe CS urged residents residing in water catchment areas to vacate or face forceful eviction.\n\nNjeru said human activities on water catchment areas have contributed to climate change effects.\n\n“Nature demands its place and as we have seen recently, the water has displaced those who encroached on wetlands, riparian lands and water catchment areas,\" he said.\n\n\"Even if the government does not remove you, water will surely force you out and it can bring about damages, losses and even deaths . I want to call upon those in such areas to move out.\"\n\nMnyazi praised the national government for the project, adding that she will table a motion in parliament to ensure that the cost of water goes down for consumers.\n\n“The water projects have been resolved after this project became complete and the Mawasco MD has just told me that there is no longer water rationing in Malindi as before,” she said.", "url": "https://www.the-star.co.ke/news/2024-06-15-state-completes-sh4-billion-water-works-in-kilifi", "published": "2024-06-15", "date_known": true, "observed": null, "location": {"geo_loc_id": "192064", "name": "Kilifi", "latitude": -3.563, "longitude": 39.644}, "image_urls": [], "author": "The Star", "read_on": "2026-11-05", "team": null, "notice": null}, "highlights": []}, "strata.research.item:P156634/sentinel2_mosaic/200577-2018-2025": {"id": "strata.research.item:P156634/sentinel2_mosaic/200577-2018-2025", "kind": "record", "tool": "research_evidence", "stratum": "What", "short_name": null, "row": {"evidence_id": "strata.research.item:P156634/sentinel2_mosaic/200577-2018-2025", "public_from": "2025-12-31", "source": {"key": "sentinel2_mosaic", "label": "Sentinel-2 cloudless mosaics", "kind": "satellite", "license": "Sentinel-2 cloudless by EOX IT Services GmbH, contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.", "url_of_source": "https://s2maps.eu"}, "kind": "satellite", "title": "Sentinel-2 cloudless mosaics of 2018 and 2025 around Baricho Sub-Location", "text": "Two grids of 3 by 3 Sentinel-2 cloudless tiles at zoom 15, centred on Baricho Sub-Location (-3.1, 39.78333): the 2018 mosaic and the 2025 mosaic. Prism reads no pixels; the grids are for a person to compare.", "url": "https://tiles.maps.eox.at/wmts/1.0.0/s2cloudless-2025_3857/default/